{"new_lineages":[{"id":"L-laws-are-declarations","scope":"language","statement":"Laws are ordinary declarations, holes until proven, and falsifiable by an instance that does not type."},{"id":"L-total-vs-partial-guarantee","scope":"language","statement":"A law can hold either by conditioning it in the type (total function, partial guarantee) or by restricting the function (partial function, total guarantee), the choice depending on who uses it."},{"id":"L-sugar-adds-no-term-constructor","scope":"language","statement":"Sugar may add nothing to Term: a construct that forces a new Term constructor is a primitive to discuss, not sugar."},{"id":"L-redundancy-crosses-coloneq","scope":"language","statement":"The redundancy worth keeping is the one that crosses the := , where type and body say different things the verifier checks; redundancy repeated on one side is removable with sugar."},{"id":"L-definition-form-depends-on-type","scope":"language","statement":"The form of a definition, how its clauses are written, may depend on reducing its type, the same definitional equality at work."},{"id":"L-numeric-tests-not-logic","scope":"method","statement":"The aim of the first version is to generate numeric tests, not to do propositional logic."},{"id":"L-invariant-is-bool-function-field","scope":"language","statement":"In version 0 an invariant is a function returning Bool, stored as a field of the definition beside the body, with a generator hole filled by type and the run kept as data."},{"id":"L-laws-like-cpp-requires","scope":"language","statement":"Laws are written in few lines like a C++ requires block: quantified variables in a header and one Bool expression per line, each a type requirement checked without running or a value requirement run by the test."},{"id":"L-concept-is-predicate-over-functions","scope":"language","statement":"A concept is a predicate over functions, a list of requirements, so two implementations satisfying it are interchangeable."},{"id":"L-no-value-without-passing-check","scope":"language","statement":"There is no way to hold a value of a subtype without its predicate having been evaluated or proved; the check runs when the value is built."},{"id":"L-laws-are-the-normal-form","scope":"language","statement":"The novelty is that a group of functions with its laws, as a type whose laws are run by default, is the normal way to define anything, not a separate verification tool."},{"id":"L-unit-is-group-of-functions","scope":"language","statement":"The unit of definition is not a function with a postcondition but a group of functions with the laws relating them; a single function is the group of size one."},{"id":"L-concept-is-a-function","scope":"language","statement":"A concept needs no new type: it is an ordinary function returning a type (with proofs) or a list of laws (with tests)."},{"id":"L-law-is-type-plus-predicate","scope":"language","statement":"A law is a type of arguments together with a predicate over them, so the generator knows what to sample and different laws can quantify over different variables."},{"id":"L-laws-force-decisions","scope":"language","statement":"Writing a group's laws forces design decisions, such as what a read of an absent key returns."},{"id":"L-almacen-is-constructor-function","scope":"language","statement":"A structure like Almacen is a function that takes the raw operations and returns the verified versions, so the only way to obtain one is to pass the check."},{"id":"L-check-value-vs-function","scope":"language","statement":"Checking a value is an exact computation and checking a function is sampling, but both are the same construct operation differing only in whether the check is exact or statistical."},{"id":"L-construction-pattern-is-core","scope":"language","statement":"The core of Luthier is one construction pattern: from a material type and a predicate over it derive a certified type, a validating constructor and the forgetful projection to the material, the same for values, functions and whole programs."},{"id":"L-certificate-first-class","scope":"language","statement":"The certificate is a first-class value carrying its level of guarantee, composable and forgettable, accumulating by composition across values, functions and programs, with runs persisted as data."},{"id":"L-luthier-is-verified-library","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier itself is a series of verified types and functions with their theorems and tests, almost an instance of the pattern."},{"id":"L-certificate-clause-with-hypotheses","scope":"language","statement":"A certificate is a clause with hypotheses (H1 and ... and Hn imply L), and a program's certificate is the closure of those clauses, which gives composition its exact meaning."},{"id":"L-name-the-pattern-library","scope":"method","statement":"The verification structure the pattern builds is named Library, not Luthier the language; an instance is a program that passed its tests and can show it, so the adjective tested is superfluous."},{"id":"L-units-with-parametrizable-requirements","scope":"language","statement":"No language ships units that carry a set of requirements they must satisfy; in Luthier the predicate is a function with parameters (max error, scalability), so the certificate states which parameters are met and a dependent library inherits them."},{"id":"L-luthier-is-extended-concepts","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier is C++ concepts extended to the whole language: predicates with semantic requirements that are run and whose results are stored, keeping subsumption and composition."},{"id":"L-evaluation-storage-object","scope":"language","statement":"The object storing a function, its argument and its result is the graph of evaluation (Ev f), with a sugar that evaluates and stores it; evalstore is just that pair, not a third operation, and the run base is a list of them."},{"id":"L-law-takes-function-as-argument","scope":"language","statement":"A law takes the function under test as its first argument and the quantified variables after, so the type it belongs to has any implementation as a term."},{"id":"L-ev-sugar-syntax","scope":"language","statement":"The sugar that stores an evaluation needs delimiters and always stores, since the context cannot decide its type and keeping only failures is the test's filter, not a property of the evaluation."},{"id":"L-symbol-economy","scope":"language","statement":"A symbol is used only for what appears often with a fixed universal meaning and already exists outside with it; anything rare, content-bearing or without a natural reading is a word, since the language is read aloud and written by hand."},{"id":"L-ascii-canonical","scope":"language","statement":"The canonical form of the language is ASCII; an editor may display -> as an arrow or * as a cross, but what is run by hand and grepped is the ASCII."},{"id":"L-predicate-concatenation","scope":"language","statement":"Predicates concatenate two ways: conjunction where both pass, and refinement where the second is defined over the subtype the first carves and the order is forced."},{"id":"L-match-is-recursor-sugar","scope":"language","statement":"match is not primitive but sugar for the recursor a data declaration gives, the only way to open a data value, and over Bool it is if."},{"id":"L-core-library-sugar-layers","scope":"language","statement":"The language is three layers: a small core (a handful of term forms plus data, definition and reduction, a ten-line EBNF with reserved words data, match, extern), a base library written in Luthier, and sugar whose translation is mechanical; no Bool, numbers, =, Sigma or Subtype in the core."},{"id":"L-predicate-delimiter-syntax","scope":"language","statement":"How to mark where a subtype's predicate begins: the bar is overloaded, so use a dedicated form rather than overload it further."},{"id":"L-set-builder-notation","scope":"language","statement":"The single subtype form is set-builder notation { pattern : material | predicate }, which denotes a Sigma; mainstream languages omit it because deciding the predicate needs a solver, a proof or a runtime check, and Luthier's fourth way tests it with n cases recorded in the certificate."},{"id":"L-candidate-named-not-forall","scope":"language","statement":"The candidate value is named and the laws applied to it explicitly, but it has the material's type (naming it by the subtype is circular) and needs no forall; the sample size is certificate data, not part of the type."},{"id":"L-coupled-functions-product","scope":"language","statement":"When a law couples several functions, the material is their product and the pattern unpacks it to name each component, so the laws are written over named components."}],
