{"new_lineages":[{"id":"L-literal-lint-type-vs-criterion","scope":"method","statement":"A mechanical criterion to classify numeric literals: a constant in type position (a refinement bound) defines a type, one in term position is a criterion."},{"id":"L-precommit-lints-not-certify","scope":"method","statement":"The pre-commit hook cannot run luthier certify (too slow, writes certificates mid-commit); it should run the lints and luthier check."},{"id":"L-hook-only-changed-files","scope":"method","statement":"A Stop hook that runs luthier check over all project files charges every turn; it should run only on changed .lut files."},{"id":"L-mechanical-rules-plan-consistency","scope":"method","statement":"Luciano doubts whether the mechanical-rules plan is consistent."},{"id":"L-check-exit-zero-unproven","scope":"method","statement":"luthier check exits with code 0 even when laws are unproven, so a Stop hook would not catch the uncovered obligations."},{"id":"L-mechanical-check-is-shadow","scope":"method","statement":"Rules that are matters of judgment transfer to a mechanical check only as a substitute, a shadow of the real rule."},{"id":"L-generators-plan-not-implemented","scope":"method","statement":"The most serious issue is that the whole generators plan is not implemented and may not even be specified."},{"id":"L-generic-functor-needs-reflection","scope":"language","statement":"Generators as values and generics as functors over the type contradict, since a functor over any type would need (T:U)->Gen T and reflection over types, which Luthier lacks."},{"id":"L-generic-functor-in-interpreter","scope":"language","statement":"Generic functors live in the interpreter, derived from the type's structure as == already is, named and recorded in the certificate but not written in Luthier."},{"id":"L-luthier-reflection-question","scope":"language","statement":"Luciano challenges the claim that Luthier has no reflection over types."},{"id":"L-typecase-breaks-parametricity","scope":"language","statement":"A match over types would break the parametricity on which proving a generic law in one instance rests."},{"id":"L-u-no-elimination","scope":"language","statement":"The universe U has introduction (every type is a value of U) but an empty elimination column: a function can use a T:U but cannot match on its shape."},{"id":"L-code-universe","scope":"language","statement":"A data Code describes the type shapes that can be generated, El decodes each to a type, and the functors become ordinary Luthier functions over codes."},{"id":"L-code-fixpoint-constructors","scope":"language","statement":"Recursive types are described with var and mu constructors as a fixpoint, with strict positivity holding for free because Code has no arrow with var on the left."},{"id":"L-code-universe-staged","scope":"language","statement":"Implement the code universe in stages: first codes without mu (numbers with bounds, products, sums, vectors, refinements), adding mu with Boltzmann sampling when a recursive data needs generating."},{"id":"L-fixpoint-generation-cost","scope":"language","statement":"Generating a value of a fixpoint needs termination control: the cost is the size and the per-size counts come from the code without looking at a value, under the same accumulated-cost rule as numbers and censuses."},{"id":"L-code-el-induction-recursion","scope":"language","statement":"Code and El are defined mutually (induction-recursion), which in Luthier's vocabulary is a cycle, i.e. an object."},{"id":"L-russell-vs-tarski-universe","scope":"language","statement":"A Russell universe is open (an element of U is a type, match can't be exhaustive) while a Tarski universe is a closed inductive data of codes over which match by induction is available."},{"id":"L-code-universe-requirements","scope":"language","statement":"The code universe asks of Luthier mutual definition of Code and El, large elimination, and dependent pattern matching, so R11 is its prerequisite."},{"id":"L-russell-plus-tarski-library","scope":"language","statement":"Keep Russell's U for everything and add a small closed Tarski universe of codes as a library for what must be treated generically."},{"id":"L-signature-shows-parametricity","scope":"language","statement":"With both universes a signature carries what it can do: (A:U)-> is parametric and cannot inspect A, (c:Code)-> may inspect the code's shape, and a typecase over U breaks parametricity silently."},{"id":"L-el-reading","scope":"language","statement":"El abbreviates elements: El c reads as the elements of what the code c names."},{"id":"L-el-vs-typeof-naming","scope":"language","statement":"typeOf would mislead since typeOf c means Code, not the type c names; for Luthier the decoder is named Decode, pairing with Code."},{"id":"L-elegant-vs-mosaic","scope":"method","statement":"A language is elegant when its constructions follow from one or two ideas and its self-interpreter fits a page, mosaic when each construction was added for a need; Luthier's spec is elegant but the interpreter has mosaic pieces the next round should remove."},{"id":"L-languages-from-theory","scope":"other","statement":"Popular languages are not better founded; now that metatheory can be machine-verified an old language can end up better founded than a new one, and Luthier is on the side born from a theory."},{"id":"L-sample-quality-is-prover-power","scope":"language","statement":"Because Luthier certifies refinement types by sampling rather than by a prover, what in other languages is the power of the prover is here the quality of the sample."},{"id":"L-luthier-fills-need","scope":"other","statement":"Can Luthier fill an existing need and let one express things not expressible in other languages?"},{"id":"L-novel-evidence-combination","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier adds no new expressiveness over Lean or Agda; its novelty is the evidence regime that joins laws in types, sampling with named generators and statistical levels, hash-tied evidence, and results carrying which laws they rest on."},{"id":"L-luthier-states-not-programs","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier should be a language to state and certify the claims of a computation done in Python or R, not a language to program in."},{"id":"L-full-to-unprovable","scope":"language","statement":"What distinguishes Luthier is carrying types-as-propositions a full to propositions that cannot be proved, so the grade of verification must be part of what belonging to a type affirms."},{"id":"L-statistical-and-deductive-tests","scope":"language","statement":"A function's type has two verification paths: a statistical test for what cannot be proved and a deductive test for what can, keeping both."},{"id":"L-optimize-needs-strong-certificates","scope":"language","statement":"Type, certification and measurement are one idea from three sides; optimizing over weak certificates is worse than not optimizing, so generators and certificate reach come before selection, which picks only among covering implementations and records the chosen one in each result's provenance."},{"id":"L-chain-interpretable-steps","scope":"method","statement":"The AI generates so much output that the only workable way to use it is to chain steps whose result you can interpret."},{"id":"L-intermediate-ai-scale","scope":"other","statement":"Is there an intermediate scale where the AI multiplies the author's effort without the result ceasing to be legible and checkable by him?"},{"id":"L-language-forces-method","scope":"method","statement":"Luthier's relevance is forcing the author and the AI to be methodical, and a program that compiles gives some assurance it is not garbage, by analogy with Lean's proofs."},{"id":"L-functions-by-hand-cheaper","scope":"method","statement":"Against his own argument: the same verified functions could be written by hand without the whole Luthier apparatus."},{"id":"L-luciano-writes-interpreter-c","scope":"method","statement":"Once the syntax is fixed, Luciano would write the Luthier interpreter himself, eventually a small core in C for speed with the rest in Luthier."},{"id":"L-passthrough-or-orchestrator","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier can be a language the code passes through or an orchestrator pointing outward, and both are fine with passage between them."},{"id":"L-monads-in-luthier-question","scope":"language","statement":"Open doubt whether monads or functors must be writable in Luthier, as Haskell has them."},{"id":"L-define-bnf-task","scope":"method","statement":"The concrete task is to understand Luthier's syntax and keep a well-defined BNF."},{"id":"L-sonoloc-written-in-luthier","scope":"method","statement":"Use sonolocalización as a real case: write it in Luthier, see it work, and execute it possibly in Python."},{"id":"L-synthesize-python-call","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier should synthesize the call to a Python function using Luthier syntax, so one can work in layers more outward or more inward of Python."},{"id":"L-reduce-to-minimum","scope":"method","statement":"AI output carries filler hard to judge; a criterion is to ask whether it can be reduced further and still work, as one minimizes an algorithm."},{"id":"L-object-classifier-laws","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier has a kind of object classifier: predicates about types that are the laws, the names."},{"id":"L-membership-construction-or-hypothesis","scope":"language","statement":"A value belongs to a type either by construction (the case is given) or by hypothesis, a construction by value that can fail."},{"id":"L-type-parametrized-by-use","scope":"language","statement":"A type can be parametrized by its conditions of use and environment: a cost of failure varying by situation and a prior probability of each parameter combination occurring, which need only be expressible now."},{"id":"L-function-as-expected-loss","scope":"language","statement":"What is left of a function is an expected loss, a posterior characterization that must be processable, expressible as a type of generator, test and cost."},{"id":"L-exterior-is-files","scope":"language","statement":"What is outside Luthier is largely files (executables, Python code, data, plots, even sensors), plus mutable memory, resources and processors."},{"id":"L-many-function-types","scope":"language","statement":"A function's type need not be unique; there can be many function types one invents, such as positive functions from reals to positive reals."},{"id":"L-cost-laws","scope":"language","statement":"Laws could be extended to go not to a boolean but to a cost: graded cost laws like a generalized unnormalized likelihood, asking how much a value costs rather than whether it complies."},{"id":"L-declarative-and-executive","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier has a declarative part (types that follow laws) and an executive part that runs commands (read a file, execute a function); the executive part can be internal or external."},{"id":"L-run-changes-states","scope":"language","statement":"Running a function is modeled as changing the states of the program and the states of the universe (a written file, a printed line, a sent message), the universe's changes also following rules."},{"id":"L-agnostic-program-universe-state","scope":"language","statement":"The language should not distinguish types that speak of the program's internal state from those that speak of the universe's state; the program should be agnostic."},{"id":"L-do-as-data-dependency","scope":"language","statement":"Haskell's do sequencing can be simulated as a data dependency (an output needing an input that doesn't exist yet), so do may not be strictly necessary."},{"id":"L-when-to-evaluate","scope":"language","statement":"An open question of evaluation strategy: for A=2+2 whether A is stored as 2+2 or as 4, since keeping the expression lets one recompute with different assumptions."},{"id":"L-evaluation-steps-memory-states","scope":"language","statement":"Evaluation is represented by the states of memory (2; 2+; 2+2; 4), possibly with pointers to a partially applied function, with syntax, range and type errors (arcsin of a string, a value out of range) caught along the way."}],
